## Design system

This project has a `default` design system (its token catalog is shown below). On every `create_modify_elements` call you choose how to work with it via the `designSystem` argument:

- `designSystem: "default"`: build against the `default` design system. Token discipline is enforced: every color, font, and scale slot is a token reference (`$color.surface`, `$spacing.md`, `$font.size.lg`, ...).
- `designSystem: "new"`: author your own design system for this work. Your first move must be a `ds_file("name") <body>` directive (before any element rows). It inherits from the `default` catalog below: override only what you want different (a brand can be 1-3 lines). After the directive lands, later calls behave like `"default"` but against your brand. Re-select it later by passing `designSystem: "<name>"`.
- `designSystem: "none"`: sovereign mode. No design-system constraints; use bare hex / numeric values freely. Token references still must resolve if you use them.

The argument is sticky: once set it persists for later calls until you change it. Absent the argument, the session stays in `"none"` mode. You cannot select the user's pre-existing brands; only `default`, `new`, `none`, or a brand you authored yourself. `create_html` does not take this argument: it is always sovereign (HTML/CSS cannot reference tokens), so use `create_modify_elements` (Blueprint) for any token-disciplined work.

Inherited catalog (the `default` design system):

ds_file("default")
  ds:v1
  // The default design system, carrying Brilliant's own visual identity.
  // Every token below is something your designs reference, so one edit
  // here ripples through the whole project.
  //
  // Comments like this reach the AI as it designs. Brief it the way you'd
  // brief a teammate: the intent behind a value, when to reach for it,
  // and what to avoid.
  //
  // DSL reference: https://brilliant.design/docs/design-system

  modes {
    theme:         [light, dark]
    density:       [comfortable, compact]
    accessibility: [standard, high-contrast, large-text]
  }

  // The four colors of the Brilliant mark: a confident blue lead, with
  // magenta, orange, and yellow in support. They are vivid on purpose, so
  // spend them sparingly: one primary action, an accent, a moment of
  // focus. Color earns attention here, it does not fill space.
  primary:    boldness(color(#0080FF))
  secondary:  boldness(color(#FF3377))
  tertiary:   boldness(color(#FF9900))
  quaternary: boldness(color(#FFDD00))

  // Neutral is the workhorse. Black, white, and greys carry nearly every
  // surface, border, and label; hierarchy and depth come from neutral
  // steps, not from hue.
  neutral:    boldness(color(oklch(55.6%, 0,     0)))

  // Tailwind's palettes, kept here as utility accents: chart series, data
  // viz, an illustration's exact hue. They are not part of Brilliant's
  // identity: they are for emphasis and data viz. Surfaces and text use the
  // color.* roles below.
  red:      boldness(color(oklch(63.7%, 0.237, 25.331)))
  orange:   boldness(color(oklch(70.5%, 0.213, 47.604)))
  amber:    boldness(color(oklch(76.9%, 0.188, 70.08)))
  yellow:   boldness(color(oklch(79.5%, 0.184, 86.047)))
  lime:     boldness(color(oklch(76.8%, 0.233, 130.85)))
  green:    boldness(color(oklch(72.3%, 0.219, 149.579)))
  emerald:  boldness(color(oklch(69.6%, 0.17,  162.48)))
  teal:     boldness(color(oklch(70.4%, 0.14,  182.503)))
  cyan:     boldness(color(oklch(71.5%, 0.143, 215.221)))
  sky:      boldness(color(oklch(68.5%, 0.169, 237.323)))
  blue:     boldness(color(oklch(62.3%, 0.214, 259.815)))
  indigo:   boldness(color(oklch(58.5%, 0.233, 277.117)))
  violet:   boldness(color(oklch(60.6%, 0.25,  292.717)))
  purple:   boldness(color(oklch(62.7%, 0.265, 303.9)))
  fuchsia:  boldness(color(oklch(66.7%, 0.295, 322.15)))
  pink:     boldness(color(oklch(65.6%, 0.241, 354.308)))
  rose:     boldness(color(oklch(64.5%, 0.246, 16.439)))
  slate:    boldness(color(oklch(55.4%, 0.046, 257.417)))
  gray:     boldness(color(oklch(55.1%, 0.027, 264.364)))
  zinc:     boldness(color(oklch(55.2%, 0.016, 285.938)))
  stone:    boldness(color(oklch(55.3%, 0.013, 58.071)))

  // Geometry. Keep spacing and sizing on these scales: consistent rhythm
  // is what makes a layout feel composed. Brilliant runs sharp and precise,
  // so heavy rounding and thick strokes are the exception, not the default.
  spacing:      tshirt(number([4, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32, 48, 64, 96, 128]),
                       min: { none: 0 })
  radius:       tshirt(number([2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64, 96]),
                       min: { none: 0 }, max: { full: 9999 },
                       transforms: none)
  stroke.width: boldness(number([0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3, 4]),
                         min: { none: 0 }, max: { thick: 8, thicker: 16, thickest: 48 })
  visibility:   boldness(number([0.05, 0.10, 0.20, 0.40, 0.50, 0.60, 0.80, 0.90, 0.95]),
                         min: { invisible: 0 }, max: { opaque: 1 })

  // Negative spacing for overlap. Auto layout accepts negative gaps, so
  // these "pull children together" instead of pushing them apart. Reach
  // for them when you want avatars in a stack, a badge biting into a
  // card corner, or content breaking out of its container.
  spacing.overlap.xs:  -2
  spacing.overlap.sm:  -4
  spacing.overlap.md:  -8
  spacing.overlap.lg:  -16
  spacing.overlap.xl:  -24
  spacing.overlap.2xl: -32

  // Type pairs two voices. Manrope is the default: a clean, modern
  // workhorse for UI, labels, and body. The serif brings warmth and
  // maturity to editorial moments, headlines, and hero text. A serif
  // headline can anchor a page; a paragraph uses one of the two faces.
  // `$font.family.sans` is an alias of the default, for when you want to
  // name the sans out loud beside `$font.family.serif`: same face, one
  // home. Retint the type by editing `font.family` and both follow.
  font.family:        Manrope
  font.family.sans:   font.family
  font.family.serif:  "Noto Serif"
  font.family.mono:   "JetBrains Mono"

  // font.size opts out of the tshirt default compact shift, shrinking
  // text by mode is a readability regression. Large-text still widens.
  font.size:          tshirt(number([12, 14, 16, 20, 24, 32, 36, 40, 48, 64, 80, 96, 128]),
                             transforms: { accessibility.large-text: shift(+1) })
  font.weight:        boldness(number([100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900]))
  font.lineHeight:    looseness(number([1.0, 1.25, 1.375, 1.5, 1.625, 2.0]))
  font.letterSpacing: looseness(number([-0.05, -0.025, -0.0125, 0, 0.025, 0.1]))

  // Semantic roles. Build interfaces from these, not from raw palette
  // stops: they re-tint with the brand and flip correctly between light
  // and dark, so a surface stays a surface and text stays legible on its
  // own. Most of a screen lives in these neutrals; the brand roles are
  // the highlights.
  color.surface:                neutral.hint
  color.surface.container:      neutral.faint
  color.surface.container.high: neutral.subtle
  color.on-surface:             neutral.strong

  // Interactive states for items sitting on `$color.surface`. Swap the role
  // in for the cursor's feedback: hover for "I see you", pressed for the
  // moment of click, selected for the current row in a nav/menu/list.
  // Disabled state uses `$color.text.disabled` on the muted label/icon
  // while the surface stays at rest, the same pattern apps actually use, no
  // extra background token needed. For items on a deeper container, step
  // one boldness role darker (e.g. `$neutral.subtle` for hover).
  color.surface.hover:          neutral.faint
  color.surface.pressed:        neutral.subtle
  color.surface.selected:       primary.hint
  color.on-surface.selected:    neutral.bold

  color.outline:                neutral.soft
  color.outline.variant:        neutral.subtle

  color.text.primary:           neutral.bold
  color.text.secondary:         neutral.firm
  color.text.disabled:          neutral.soft
  color.text.display:           primary.firm
  color.text.display.alt:       secondary.firm

  color.primary:                primary.mid
  color.on-primary:             neutral.50
  color.primary.container:      primary.hint

  color.secondary:              secondary.mid
  color.on-secondary:           neutral.50
  color.secondary.container:    secondary.hint

  color.tertiary:               tertiary.mid
  color.on-tertiary:            neutral.50
  color.tertiary.container:     tertiary.hint

  color.quaternary:             quaternary.mid
  color.on-quaternary:          neutral.900     // dark text: yellow is too bright to carry white
  color.quaternary.container:   quaternary.hint

  color.success:                green.mid
  color.on-success:             neutral.50
  color.success.container:      green.hint

  color.error:                  red.mid
  color.on-error:               neutral.50
  color.error.container:        red.hint

  color.warning:                orange.mid
  color.on-warning:             neutral.900     // dark text: orange needs a dark foreground too
  color.warning.container:      orange.hint

  color.info:                   blue.mid
  color.on-info:                neutral.50
  color.info.container:         blue.hint

  // shadow and glow take absolute colors on purpose: a shadow stays dark
  // whether it falls on a light or dark surface. Shadows are painted with
  // $color.shadow. A brand or neutral role here flips in dark mode and
  // turns the shadow into a glowing halo.
  color.shadow:                 neutral.950
  color.glow:                   neutral.50

  // Composites bundle several atoms into one decision: a full heading
  // style, a layered shadow. typography.h1 and shadow.md are those composites,
  // set once instead of re-assembling size, weight, and blur each time.
  typography.display.lg: { fontSize: font.size.6xl, fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.1  }
  typography.display.md: { fontSize: 52,            fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.1  }
  typography.display.sm: { fontSize: 44,            fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.15 }

  typography.h1: { fontSize: font.size.3xl, fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.2  }
  typography.h2: { fontSize: 30,            fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.25 }
  typography.h3: { fontSize: font.size.xl,  fontWeight: font.weight.firm, lineHeight: 1.3  }
  typography.h4: { fontSize: font.size.lg,  fontWeight: font.weight.firm, lineHeight: 1.35 }

  typography.body.lg: { fontSize: 18,             fontWeight: font.weight.soft, lineHeight: font.lineHeight.normal }
  typography.body.md: { fontSize: font.size.md,   fontWeight: font.weight.soft, lineHeight: font.lineHeight.normal }
  typography.body.sm: { fontSize: font.size.sm,   fontWeight: font.weight.soft, lineHeight: font.lineHeight.normal }

  typography.button:  { fontSize: font.size.sm,   fontWeight: font.weight.firm, lineHeight: font.lineHeight.tight }
  typography.input:   { fontSize: font.size.md,   fontWeight: font.weight.soft, lineHeight: 1.4 }
  typography.label:   { fontSize: font.size.sm,   fontWeight: font.weight.mid,  lineHeight: 1.4 }
  typography.caption: { fontSize: font.size.xs,   fontWeight: font.weight.soft, lineHeight: 1.4 }
  typography.code:    { fontSize: font.size.sm,   fontWeight: font.weight.soft,
                         lineHeight: font.lineHeight.normal, fontFamily: font.family.mono }

  typography.editorial.lg: { fontSize: font.size.6xl, fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.1,  fontFamily: font.family.serif }
  typography.editorial.md: { fontSize: 52,            fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.1,  fontFamily: font.family.serif }
  typography.editorial.sm: { fontSize: 44,            fontWeight: font.weight.bold, lineHeight: 1.15, fontFamily: font.family.serif }

  shadow.2xs: [ drop(y: 1, blur: 0, color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05)) ]
  shadow.xs:  [ drop(y: 1, blur: 1, color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05)) ]
  shadow.sm:  [ drop(y: 1, blur: 2, color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.05)) ]

  shadow.md: [
